  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $4c+5=3yc-2,$ then $c=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate all terms with $c$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $5$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c+5 & =3yc-2 \\\\ 4c+5-5 & =3yc-2-5 \\\\ 4c & =3yc-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3yc$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c & =3yc-7 \\\\ 4c-3yc & =3yc-7-3yc \\\\ 4c-3yc & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $c$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $c$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $c$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4c-3yc & =-7 \\\\ c(4-3y) & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4-3y)$ to isolate $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(4-3y) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{c(4-3y)}{4-3y} & =\\frac{-7}{4-3y} \\\\ \\frac{c\\cancel{(4-3y)}}{\\cancel{4-3y}} & =-\\frac{7}{4-3y} \\\\ c & =\\frac{7}{3y-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3y-4\\ne 0.$",
